# SYNTAX TEST "text.html.tiddlywiki5" "A single-line define ends at its line, parentheses or not"

# TiddlyWiki reads a define with no closing pragma as a one-line definition; the body runs to the
# end of the line and the tiddler continues beneath it.
#
# A body ENDING IN A CLOSING PARENTHESIS leaves the cursor after a parenthesis at end of line,
# which is exactly what the multiline branch waits for — its begin reads (?<=\))\s*$ — so that
# branch opens there and swallows the rest of the file hunting an \end that never comes.
# TiddlyWiki own documentation carries the shape: filters/examples/search-replace Operator
# (Examples) defines a regular-expression macro whose body reads as a group, and every line of
# prose below it colours as macro body.
#
# Anchoring the multiline branch to the parameter list with \G cures this specimen and breaks
# the genuine block form, whose parameter close is not always the match immediately preceding.
# The cure wants a discriminator made at the define opener, where the whole line can be read at
# once, rather than a guard on the branch.

! A body of plain text ends at the line

\define plain() e|o

Prose after a plain body.
#<- text.html.tiddlywiki5 meta.paragraph.tiddlywiki5
#<- - meta.variable.macro.body.tiddlywiki5

! A body ending in a closing parenthesis ends there too

\define grouped() (abc)(def)

Prose after a grouped body.
#<- text.html.tiddlywiki5 meta.paragraph.tiddlywiki5
#<- - meta.variable.macro.body.tiddlywiki5

! And a define with no body on its line still opens a block

\define blockform()
the body
\end

Prose after the block.
#<- text.html.tiddlywiki5 meta.paragraph.tiddlywiki5
#<- - meta.variable.macro.body.tiddlywiki5

